     7	# Abstract (exemplar — calibrates every later section)
     8	
     9	When a tariff can be legally dead before it stops being collected,
    10	"what is the statutory rate?" needs a date, an instrument, and a proof.
    11	We encode the 2025–26 United States tariff stack as dated, executable
    12	rules: column rates by tariff-schedule revision plus the §232, §301,
    13	§201, §338, §122, and IEEPA overlays, including the IEEPA termination
    14	order's own two endpoints ("no longer in effect" and "no longer
    15	collected"). Every rule version cites its Federal Register instrument
    16	or tariff-schedule heading with a verbatim excerpt, and terminations
    17	are themselves encoded law rather than deletions. We validate the
    18	encoding against the leading independent reference, Yale Budget Lab's
    19	tariff-rate-tracker, on the tracker's own line-by-country-by-date
    20	spine: at the pinned snapshot (dc1da3af), every in-scope cell is
    21	exactly matched or carries an evidence-backed disposition, with zero
    22	unexplained disagreements. The comparison surfaced two tracker defects,
    23	a §201 rate carried past its proclamation's staged schedule and missing
    24	column-2 handling for four countries; we reported both upstream with
    25	the legal derivations. We also release synthetic transaction-level
    26	customs entries that reproduce official Census import margins to the
    27	integer dollar: eighteen months reconciled against the publisher's own
    28	control totals with zero failures, 108,977 cells cross-checked exactly
    29	over a second official channel, and every distributional assumption
    30	registered beside the data. AI agents built the encodings and datasets
    31	behind compile, test, and oracle gates, with independent cross-model
    32	adversarial review run to zero findings. All code, corpora, and
    33	evidence are public and pinned.
    34	
    35	
    36	# 1. Why 2026 broke tariff lookup
    37	
    38	On February 20, 2026, the additional ad valorem duties imposed under
    39	the International Emergency Economic Powers Act stopped being law. They
    40	did not stop being collected. That morning the Supreme Court held that
    41	"IEEPA does not authorize the President to impose tariffs" (*Learning
    42	Resources, Inc. v. Trump*, 607 U.S. 229 (2026)); the same day,
    43	Executive Order 14389 ordered that the duties "shall no longer be in
    44	effect and, as soon as practicable, shall no longer be collected" —
    45	and named no date for the second clause. The date arrived two days
    46	later in a Customs and Border Protection cargo-systems message (CSMS
    47	#67834313), instructing that collection would end for goods entered on
    48	or after 12:00 a.m. on February 24. So the legal end of the largest
    49	tariff action of the era is a court decision and an executive order,
    50	and its operational end is an agency bulletin — three documents, two
    51	dates, four days apart. Any dataset that records "the tariff on
    52	Chinese imports in February 2026" as a single number has taken a
    53	position on which date counts, and most datasets do not say which
    54	position they took.
    55	
    56	That week is the 2025–26 tariff environment in miniature. Over
    57	eighteen months, the United States imposed, modified, litigated, and
    58	unwound overlapping duties under at least seven distinct authorities.
    59	Alongside the IEEPA orders and their termination: Section 232
    60	national-security actions across metals and manufactured goods;
    61	Section 301 actions including a forced-labor program that determined
    62	sixty investigations actionable at once, inserting a new chapter-99
    63	note into the tariff schedule (U.S. note 52) with two rate tiers and
    64	country-specific quotas; a Section 122 balance-of-payments surcharge
    65	(Proclamation 11012) that ran at 10 percent for exactly its statutory
    66	maximum of 150 days; Section 201 safeguards on solar equipment
    67	stepping down annually on a proclamation schedule to a 2026 expiry;
    68	and Section 338 duties. The seams between authorities are themselves
    69	dated facts: the §122 surcharge expired at 12:01 a.m. on July 24,
    70	2026 — the same day the forced-labor duties took effect. The tariff
    71	schedule these overlays modify was a moving target in its own right:
    72	the International Trade Commission's 2026 edition stood at Revision 15
    73	by the first week of August, and a revision can carry a single
    74	surgical change — Revision 15's only entry amends one chapter-99
    75	heading, zeroing the Section 232 rate on United Kingdom patented
    76	pharmaceuticals from +10 percent, four days after Revision 14.
    77	
    78	The result is that the object researchers call "the statutory tariff
    79	rate" became a function of four arguments — tariff line, partner
    80	country, date, and legal interpretation — and the fourth argument is
    81	the one nobody's spreadsheet has a column for. A rate query for a
    82	solar module from Malaysia on February 22, 2026 has defensible answers
    83	that differ by authority and by reading: the IEEPA overlay is legally
    84	dead but still being collected for two more days, and the safeguard
    85	duty's staged schedule has been expired for sixteen days. The
    86	difference between the answers is not a data error. It is a fact
    87	about how each authority ended, and an analysis of legal exposure
    88	needs different dates than an analysis of customs revenue.
    89	
    90	Reference datasets handled this churn the way reference datasets
    91	always have: with expert judgment applied by hand, encoded in
    92	scripts, and published as numbers whose reasoning lives in commit
    93	messages if it lives anywhere. The best of these — Yale Budget Lab's
    94	tariff-rate-tracker, an open-source pipeline that resolves the churn
    95	into an interval-encoded panel of statutory rates by tariff line,
    96	country, and date, and the validation reference we use throughout
    97	this paper — is very good. It is also the product of judgment calls
    98	that a reader cannot mechanically audit: which proclamation governs a
    99	line on a date, when a staged schedule ends, which countries a
   100	general note diverts to a different rate column. Sections 3 and 4
   101	show what happens when those calls are made checkable — including two
   102	places where the best available reference and the underlying
   103	instruments disagree.
   104	
   105	Our claim in this paper is narrow and, we think, timely: when the law
   106	moves this fast, the honest representation of a tariff rate is not a
   107	number but a dated derivation — a rule that cites the instrument that
   108	set it, bounded by the instrument that ended it, executable so that
   109	"the rate on date X" is computed rather than transcribed. The 2025–26
   110	shock is the demonstration that this is not a formalist's luxury.
   111	When rates change by proclamation monthly, die in two stages, and
   112	expire into seams that begin the next program the same morning, the
   113	derivation *is* the data.
   114	
   115	# 2. What a tariff change is, formally
   116	
   117	Every rule in our encoding is a list of dated versions. A version has
   118	an `effective_from` date, a formula or value, and a proof: one or more
   119	atoms, each citing a path into a signed corpus of legal instruments and
   120	quoting the operative text verbatim. A policy change appends a version;
   121	it never edits one. The history is not metadata attached to the
   122	representation — the history is the representation, and the engine
   123	answers "the rate on date X" by selecting the version in force at X.
   124	
   125	Three properties follow, and each one earns its keep in the 2025–26
   126	record.
   127	
   128	**Changes cannot outrun their instruments.** A version's proof atoms
   129	cite corpus paths — a Federal Register document, a tariff-schedule
   130	heading at a specific revision — and the corpus is ingested under
   131	signed manifests before any encoding can reference it. There is no way
   132	to encode "the §232 aluminum rate doubled" ahead of possessing the
   133	proclamation that doubled it, and the citation carries an excerpt, so
   134	a reviewer checks the claim against quoted text rather than against a
   135	maintainer's memory.
   136	
   137	**Endings are law, not deletions.** The IEEPA termination that opened
   138	Section 1 is encoded as a rule, not as the removal of one:
   139	
   140	```yaml
   141	# source: rulespec-us us/policies/usitc/us-tariff-duty/overlays/ieepa/termination.yaml@main (excerpt)
   142	- name: terminated_ieepa_additional_ad_valorem_duty_rate
   143	  kind: parameter
   144	  dtype: Rate
   145	  source: Executive Order 14389 section 1
   146	  metadata:
   147	    proof:
   148	      atoms:
   149	        - path: versions[0].formula
   150	          kind: parameter
   151	          source:
   152	            corpus_citation_path: us/rulemaking/federal-register/2026-02-25/2026-03832
   153	            excerpt: "shall no longer be in effect"
   154	  versions:
   155	    - effective_from: '2026-02-20'
   156	      formula: |-
   157	        0
   158	```
   159	
   160	The terminated rate is a zero that took effect on February 20, 2026,
   161	citing the order that zeroed it — which means the panel this encoding
   162	generates carries the termination as a dated fact, exactly as it
   163	carries the imposition. The two-endpoint character of the IEEPA wind-
   164	down survives into the data because both endpoints are just versions.
   165	A companion test evaluates the composed rates on the one-day period of
   166	February 20 itself and asserts that the additional duty no longer
   167	adds; the test is mandatory, so the change could not have merged
   168	without pinning its own boundary.
   169	
   170	**Schedule churn is encoded, not smoothed.** When the trade commission
   171	issues a revision that restructures lines — consolidating headings,
   172	retiring statistical suffixes — that restructuring is itself a dated
   173	module (the aluminum family, for instance, carries a
   174	revision-12 consolidation module alongside its rate overlays). A
   175	downstream user never faces the silent question of which revision's
   176	line structure a number refers to; the revision boundary is in the
   177	derivation.
   178	
   179	The full stack composes from these pieces. A composition module wires
   180	the column rates of the tariff schedule to the authority overlays —
   181	the Section 232 metal programs, the Section 301 families including the
   182	forced-labor program, the per-country IEEPA reciprocal modules and
   183	their fentanyl-order siblings, Section 122, Section 201, Section 338 —
   184	and the engine evaluates the composed rate for a (line, country, date)
   185	triple by resolving every overlay's version in force. Nothing in the
   186	pipeline is hand-transcribed from a secondary source: the corpus
   187	snapshot for this work comprises 138 signed versions — 47 Federal
   188	Register instruments, 44 tariff-schedule revision snapshots, and 47
   189	chapter-99 and general-note extractions — and every operative number
   190	in the panel traces to one of them.
   191	
   192	We do not claim this representation is novel as an idea; executable
   193	encodings of statutes have a literature (Section 8), and dated
   194	parameters are standard in tax microsimulation. The claim is about
   195	fit: 2025–26 tariff law is the environment where the alternatives
   196	visibly fail, because the thing being represented changed character.
   197	A rate that steps down on a proclamation schedule, dies in two stages,
   198	or applies to a line that stopped existing mid-year is not a cell in a
   199	table. It is a small legal argument, and the representation that
   200	survives contact with it is the one that stores the argument.
   201	
   202	# 3. Conformance against an independent tracker
   203	
   204	An encoding of tariff law can be internally consistent and wrong. The
   205	validation that matters is external: derive the panel our rules imply
   206	and reconcile it, cell by cell, against a reference built by people
   207	who never saw our code. Yale Budget Lab's tariff-rate-tracker is the
   208	strongest available reference — an actively maintained pipeline that
   209	resolves the 2025–26 churn into statutory rate columns on a tariff-
   210	line × country × date spine, with documented policy parameters and
   211	sourced instrument files. We pinned its repository at one commit
   212	(c4307e51), built its panel locally (1 hour 46 minutes wall time;
   213	123.4 GiB peak memory footprint — an envelope we measured and reported
   214	upstream, since the project's documentation suggested a much smaller
   215	machine would do), and made its spine the conformance universe.
   216	
   217	That last clause carries the methodology. The comparison universe is
   218	generated from the reference's own spine — every tariff line, partner,
   219	and date interval the tracker covers, not a curated sample of cells we
   220	expected to match. The conformance predicate is unforgiving in the
   221	same way: the encoding is conformant only when covered cells equal
   222	in-scope cells, unexplained disagreements equal zero, and open
   223	disagreements attributed to our encoding equal zero. There is no
   224	partial credit and no averaging; a single unexplained cell fails the
   225	predicate. Scope is declared, not discovered: the comparison covers
   226	the tracker's statutory rate columns — the column rates and each
   227	authority overlay, at the tracker's default legal-date convention —
   228	and excludes its estimation-layer constructs (trade-share weightings,
   229	utilization adjustments), which are modeling rather than law and are
   230	validated at a different layer of our stack.
   231	
   232	At the pinned snapshot (axiom-oracles report dc1da3af, 2026-08-03),
   233	the predicate holds: ten of ten in-scope policies covered, zero
   234	unexplained disagreements, zero open encoding-attributed
   235	disagreements. The residue is disclosed rather than hidden. Four
   236	exclusions are tripwired — carved out of scope with machine-checked
   237	guards that fail the build if the excluded condition's footprint
   238	moves, so an exclusion cannot quietly grow. 8,283 cells where the
   239	disagreement is attributed to the reference are classified and carried
   240	with evidence (Section 4 examines the two most consequential). And

