tracker’s internals (we find no formal citation of the tool itself as of August 2026), and Section
4’s derivation-backed defect reports are what that engagement produces when a disagreement
survives audit.
Rules as code and calculator validation. Executable encodings of law have an established
line: OpenFisca’s deployed tax-benefit engines [openfisca], Catala’s formally grounded statuteto-code language [merigoux2021catala], and the policy agenda articulated for rules-as-code generally [mohun2020cracking]. Cross-validation is likewise institutionalized practice in tax microsimulation — NBER’s TAXSIM as a reference implementation [feenberg1993introduction], EUROMOD’s country-report validation regime [sutherland2013euromod], and maintained harnesses
that reconcile independent calculators line-by-line [taxcalculator_taxsim_validation]. That culture has not previously reached trade law. Tariffs are, if anything, the easier target for it —
the instruments are public, the schedule is versioned by the issuing agency, and the reference
tracker already exists — and the 2025–26 record supplies the volatility that makes the exercise
informative rather than ornamental.
Synthetic microdata. The synthetic-data tradition in oﬀicial statistics runs through disclosure avoidance: synthetic establishment panels standing in for confidential business registers [kinney2011towards], noise-infused releases with formal privacy guarantees [machanavajjhala2008privacy], and, in transport and demography, synthetic populations fit to published
margins [beckman1996creating]. Customs data specifically have seen synthesis for machinelearning research — artificial Korean import declarations generated for fraud-detection benchmarks [jeong2022customs] — while the true U.S. transaction record remains restricted-access
[bernard2009importers; census_lfttd] or proprietary. Our entries invert the disclosure-avoidance
objective: the inputs are already public, no confidentiality constraint binds, and the generator’s
contract is exact integer reproduction of every published margin cell with a declared assumptions register below that grain. We find no prior public U.S. synthetic customs-entry dataset
with that contract — the combination this paper ships (instrument-cited executable rules, a
conformance verdict against an independent implementation, and margin-exact public entries)
appears to be unoccupied, and we state the boundary of that claim as a search bounded at
August 2026 rather than a certainty.

Data and code availability
Everything this paper reports predates it in public. The encodings live in the rulespec-us
repository (the tariff composition and its authority overlays, with mandatory companion tests);
the corpus manifests, comparison harness, conformance reports, and disposition records in axiomoracles, with the quoted scoreboard pinned at snapshot dc1da3af; the entries pipeline, its 149-test
battery, and the committed evidence packs (build report, source manifest, eighteen per-month
reconciliation records, the gated cross-check report) in the Microcosm repository, merged as
pull requests #620 and #621 with the full adversarial review inventories in their descriptions.
The Yale reference is pinned at commit c4307e51 of Budget-Lab-Yale/ tariff-rate-tracker; our
six upstream reports are public issues on that repository. The Census source archives are public
no-auth downloads whose URL pattern, sizes, and sha-256 digests appear in the committed
source manifest; the CBP page bytes our anchors parse from are archived beside the facts they
ground.
Replication is therefore not a package assembled for this paper but the working state of the
